For OLS regression we have that,

\begin{equation*}
    \hat{\beta}_{OLS} = \underset{\beta}{\text{argmin}} \: ||Y - X\beta||^{2}
\end{equation*}

$$$$

For fitting regression via the MLE, we have the following model,

\begin{align*}
    Y = X\beta + \epsilon, \: \epsilon \sim N(0, I_{p} \sigma^{2})
\end{align*}

and the goal will be to maximize the log-likelihood of the data where $\beta$ and $\sigma^{2}$ are unknown parameters. We thus have,

\begin{align*}
    \hat{\beta}_{MLE} &= \underset{\beta}{\text{argmax}} \: \log(L(\beta, \sigma^{2})) \\
    &= \underset{\beta}{\text{argmax}} \: \frac{-n}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^{2}) - \frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}||Y-X\beta||^{2} \\
    &= \underset{\beta}{\text{argmax}} \: -\frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}||Y-X\beta||^{2} \\
    &= \underset{\beta}{\text{argmin}} \: \frac{1}{2\sigma^{2}}||Y-X\beta||^{2} \\
    &= \underset{\beta}{\text{argmin}} \: ||Y-X\beta||^{2} 
\end{align*}

which follows from the fact that if we want to maximize with respect to $\beta$, we can ignore any terms that don't depend on $\beta$, and that maximizing a quantity is equivalent to minimizing the negative of that same quantity.

$$$$

We can thus see that no matter how we fit the regression model, we end up minimizing the same quantity with respect to $\beta$. Thus, $\hat{\beta}_{OLS} = \hat{\beta}_{MLE}$ and option (3) is the correct answer.

