Before beginning any calculations, we should first check if this sort of hypothesis can even be tested via an $F$ and $T$ test. Since we're testing the value of a single coefficient, it is clear that a $T$ test is appropriate. What about an $F$ test? Recall that an $F$ test is used to test the fit of one "large" model to a "smaller" model which is a subset of the large one. In this case, the "large" model would be $Y = \beta_{0} + X\beta_{1}$ and the "small" model would be $Y = \beta_{0}$, where the "small" model is a subset of the large model (specifically with $\beta_{1} = 0$. Thus, an $F$ test can be used and we can move on to our calculations. Note that this means Options (6) and (7) cannot be correct.

