The $t-$statistic for testing $H_{0}: \beta_{1} = 0$ is given by,
\begin{align*}
    T_{s} &= \frac{ \hat{\beta_{1}} }{ SE(\hat{\beta}_{1}) } \\
\end{align*}
where $SE(\hat{\beta}_{1})$ is the standard error of $\hat{\beta_{1}}$ and is equal to,
\begin{align*}
    SE(\hat{\beta}_{1}) &= \sqrt{ \frac{ \frac{ \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \hat{Y}_{i})^{2} }{n - 2} } { \sum_{i=1}^{n}(\bar{X}  - X_{i})^{2} }  } = \sqrt{ \frac{ \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \hat{Y}_{i})^{2} }{(n - 2)\sum_{i=1}^{n}(\bar{X}  - X_{i})^{2}} } 
\end{align*}
$$$$
The $F-$statistic for testing $H_{0}: \beta_{1} = 0$ is given by,
\begin{align*}
    F_{s} = \frac{ RSS_{1} - RSS_{2} }{ RSS_{2} } \cdot \frac{ n - p_{2 }}{ p_{2} - p_{1}}
\end{align*}
where $RSS_{1}$ is the residual sum of squares of the model without $\beta_{1}$, $RSS_{2}$ is the residual sum of squares of the model with $\beta_{1}$, $n$ is the total number of datapoints, $p_{1}$ is the number of predictors in the model without $\beta_{1}$ (including the intercept), and $p_{2}$ is the number of predictors in the model with $\beta_{1}$ (including the intercept).

$$$$

Plugging these values in for our scenario yields,
\begin{align*}
    F_{s} &= \frac{ RSS_{1} - RSS_{2} }{ RSS_{2} } \cdot \frac{ n - 2}{ 2 - 1} \\
    &= (n-2) \frac{ RSS_{1} - RSS_{2} }{ RSS_{2} }
\end{align*}

Our task now is to input the correct terms for $RSS_{1}$ and $RSS_{2}$, and then simplify the expression. First, in the below it will be helpful to remember that in the linear regression model with an intercept and one extra predictor, we have, $\hat{\beta}_{0} = \bar{Y} - \hat{\beta}_{1}\bar{X}$. Beginning with the numerator, we have,
\begin{align*}
    RSS_{1} - RSS_{2} &= \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \hat{\alpha}_{0})^{2} - \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \hat{\beta}_{0} - \hat{\beta}_{1}X_{i})^{2} \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \bar{Y})^{2} - \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - (\bar{Y} - \hat{\beta_{1}}\bar{X} ) - \hat{\beta}_{1}X_{i})^{2} \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_{i} - \bar{Y})^{2} - \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( (Y_{i} - \bar{Y})  + \hat{\beta_{1}} (\bar{X}  - X_{i}) \right)^{2} \\
