The PMF of a $\bern{\theta}$ distribution is $p(k \mid \theta) = \theta^k(1-\theta)^{1-k}$ for $k = 0,1$. Therefore, the likelihood function and log-likelihood functio, respectively, are given by 

\begin{align*}
    L(\theta) &= \displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^n p(x_i \mid \theta) = \prod_{i=1}^n \theta^{x_i}(1-\theta)^{1-x_i} = \theta^{\sum x_i}(1-\theta)^{n - \sum x_i} \\
    l(\theta) &= \log(L(\theta)) = \log\left(\theta^{\sum x_i}(1-\theta)^{n - \sum x_i}\right) = \log(\theta) \sum x_i + \log(1-\theta)\left(n - \sum x_i\right)
\end{align*}

We can take the partial derivative of $l(\theta)$ to solve for the MLE. This yields 
\begin{align*}
    \partial l/\partial \theta =& \frac{\sum x_i}{\theta} - \frac{n-\sum x_i}{1-\theta} = 0\\
    \iff& (1-\theta)\sum x_i - \theta\left(n - \sum x_i\right) = 0\\
    \iff& \theta = \dfrac{\sum x_i}{n}
\end{align*}

Thus, the correct answer is Option (3).

The Normal distribution has two parameters, but we are assuming $\mu$ is known, so there is really only one unknown parameter here. The PDF of a $N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ distribution is 
\begin{align*}
f(x \mid \mu,\sigma^2) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\right)^2}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the likelihood and log-likelihood are given by 

\begin{align*}
    L(\mu) &= \displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i \mid \mu,\sigma^2) = \prod_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{x_i-\mu}{\sigma}\right)^2} = \left(\dfrac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}\right)^n e^{-\frac{1}{2}\sum \left(\frac{x_i - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^2} \\
    \\
    l(\mu) &= \log(L(\mu)) = \log\left(\left(\dfrac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}\right)^n e^{-\frac{1}{2}\sum \left(\frac{x_i - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^2} \right) = -\dfrac{n}{2}\log(2\pi \sigma^2) - \dfrac{1}{2}\sum \left(\frac{x_i - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^2
\end{align*}
