Recall that an Exponential random variable, $X$, has PDF
\begin{align*}
    f(x \mid \lambda) = \lambda e^{-\lambda x}
\end{align*}
Importantly, the support of the Exponential distribution does not depend on $\theta$. This means that we can calculate the Fisher Information for $\lambda$ (for a sample size of $n=1$) using the formula
\begin{align*}
    I_{1}(\lambda) = -E[\dfrac{\partial^2]{\partial \lambda^2}\left(\log(f(X \mid \lambda))\right)}
\end{align*}
First, taking the log, we get
\begin{align*}
    \log(f(x \mid \lambda)) = \log(\lambda) - \lambda x
\end{align*}
Calculating the derivatives yields
\begin{align*}
    \dfrac{\partial}{\partial \lambda}\left(\log(f(x \mid \lambda))\right) = \dfrac{1}{\lambda} - x\\
    \dfrac{\partial^2}{\partial \lambda^2}\left(\log(f(x \mid \lambda))\right) = \dfrac{-1}{\lambda^2}
\end{align*}
Thus, the Fisher Information (again, in the case that $n=1$) is
\begin{align*}
I_{1}(\theta) &= -E[\dfrac{-1]{\lambda^2}} \\
&= \dfrac{1}{\lambda^2} \\
\end{align*}
Recall that this is just the Fisher Information for $\lambda$ when we have a single $X$. As mentioned at the beginning of the solution, we can simply multiply this by $n$ to get the final result. Thus, the Fisher Information is
\begin{align*}
    I(\lambda) = \dfrac{n}{\lambda^2}
\end{align*}
Plugging in the given numbers from the question yields that the final result is $16/3^{2} =  16/9$.

